    objectives: "• Use surrounding words to infer meaning of unfamiliar vocabulary.\n• Explain the idea with a clear example, annotation, or revision.\n• Check work: evidence pointer, agreement/clarity, or context-clue verification.",
    content: "# What Context Clues Do\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 1 of 16 · Vocabulary Power · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use surrounding words to infer meaning of unfamiliar vocabulary.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nCircle any unfamiliar word in this sentence and guess its meaning from neighbors: “The ranger’s concise warning — short, clear, and urgent — kept hikers from cutting the muddy switchback.”\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 1 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nStrong readers do not stop at “I don’t know that word.” They hunt for **context clues** and **word parts** (roots, prefixes, suffixes). Today’s skill — **What Context Clues Do** — builds that detective habit.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Context clue** = hints in the same sentence or nearby sentences.\n- **Root / affix** = word parts that carry meaning across many words.\n- **Connotation** = the feeling a word carries beyond its dictionary sense.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a practice for the spring showcase. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 10, set 4.\n\n**Mentor passage:**\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the task** in your own words (main idea? craft? integration?).\n**Step 2 — Annotate:** underline claims, circle key words, star evidence.\n**Step 3 — Answer with a complete sentence** that includes a pointer to the text.\n**Step 4 — Check:** Could a partner find your evidence in under 10 seconds?\n\n**Sample response quality:** “The central idea is that careful hikers protect trails by slowing down at muddy markers, which is supported by the detail that most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “What Context Clues Do,” but switch the context to a Prosper Prep morning advisory. Use a fresh short example (numbers 4 and 23 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** switching verb tense mid-paragraph for no reason.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nVocabulary and key-idea reading feed each other: you cannot summarize well if key words stay foggy.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Using the mentor passage above, apply “What Context Clues Do.” Underline evidence.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Answer must point to a specific line or phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Partner challenge: write one wrong answer that looks tempting, then explain the trap.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Naming traps builds metacognition.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. In “The speaker’s **candid** apology — honest and unpolished — surprised the room,” what does *candid* most nearly mean? Underline the clue words.\n2. “Unlike her **verbose** partner, Nina gave a two-sentence update.” Infer *verbose* and name the clue type.\n3. Write an original sentence using *fragile* with a clear definition clue. Mark the clue.\n4. Which clue helps more in “He felt **exhausted** after the double practice”? Nearby synonym or contrast? Invent a stronger rewrite with an explicit clue.\n5. Circle the unfamiliar word and list two neighbor words that help: “The **arid** field, dry and cracked, needed rain.”\n6. Partner trap: Write a sentence where *bright* could mean “smart” OR “shiny.” Then add one clue that locks the meaning to “smart.”\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. Honest / frank / straightforward. Clue: “honest and unpolished” (definition/restatement).\n2. Wordy / talkative. Contrast clue: “Unlike… two-sentence.”\n3. Sample: “The fragile vase — easily broken — sat on a high shelf.” Clue = dash definition.\n4. Current sentence has weak context. Stronger: “He felt exhausted, completely worn out, after double practice.”\n5. *arid*; clues “dry and cracked.” Meaning: very dry.\n6. Sample lock: “Her bright solution — clever and new — fixed the schedule.”\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “What Context Clues Do” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to switching verb tense mid-paragraph for no…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about the mentor passage that depends on “What Context Clues Do.” Label C / E / R in the margin. Then invent a harder follow-up question for a classmate.\n",
