    content: "# Inferring Character Motivation\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 2 of 16 · Reading: Key Ideas and Details · Lesson 4*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use dialogue and action to infer why a character acts.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSkim this mini-passage once, then write one sentence naming what it seems to be mostly about:\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.…\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 1 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nReading is thinking with a text. Today’s skill — **Inferring Character Motivation** — sits inside Reading: Key Ideas and Details. We will slow down, annotate, and back every claim with evidence a classmate could find.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Main idea / central idea** = what the text is mostly saying (complete sentence).\n- **Evidence** = quotation or paraphrase you can point to.\n- **Inference** = a conclusion backed by text + reasoning (not a random guess).\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a practice for the spring showcase. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 8, set 11.\n\n**Mentor passage:**\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the task** in your own words (main idea? craft? integration?).\n**Step 2 — Annotate:** underline claims, circle key words, star evidence.\n**Step 3 — Answer with a complete sentence** that includes a pointer to the text.\n**Step 4 — Check:** Could a partner find your evidence in under 10 seconds?\n\n**Sample response quality:** “The central idea is that careful hikers protect trails by slowing down at muddy markers, which is supported by the detail that most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Inferring Character Motivation,” but switch the context to a scholarship essay workshop. Use a fresh short example (numbers 7 and 15 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** stuffing a sentence with adjectives instead of choosing one precise noun.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nVocabulary and key-idea reading feed each other: you cannot summarize well if key words stay foggy.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Using the mentor passage above, apply “Inferring Character Motivation.” Underline evidence.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Answer must point to a specific line or phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Partner challenge: write one wrong answer that looks tempting, then explain the trap.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Naming traps builds metacognition.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n2. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n3. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n4. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n5. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n6. Using Maya or Jordan (depending on excerpt), infer a motivation in one sentence and quote/paraphrase one action or line that supports it. Excerpt start: “Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did n…”\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n2. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n3. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n4. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n5. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n6. Inference + specific evidence (e.g., Maya delays opening → anxiety/importance; Jordan uses study-hall breath → seeks calm).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Inferring Character Motivation” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to stuffing a sentence with adjectives instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about the mentor passage that depends on “Inferring Character Motivation.” Label C / E / R in the margin. Then invent a harder follow-up question for a classmate.\n",
