    objectives: "• Quote or paraphrase the exact lines that answer a question.\n• Explain the idea with a clear example, annotation, or revision.\n• Check work: evidence pointer, agreement/clarity, or context-clue verification.",
    content: "# Finding Explicit Evidence\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 2 of 16 · Reading: Key Ideas and Details · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** quote or paraphrase the exact lines that answer a question.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSkim this mini-passage once, then write one sentence naming what it seems to be mostly about:\n\n> Trail crews near Tyler mark muddy sections with simple wooden signs. Hikers who slow down at those markers protect both the path and the plants beside it. A short pause also helps younger walkers notice roots and loose rocks. Rangers say most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile back to the parking lot.…\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 1 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nReading is thinking with a text. Today’s skill — **Finding Explicit Evidence** — sits inside Reading: Key Ideas and Details. We will slow down, annotate, and back every claim with evidence a classmate could find.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Main idea / central idea** = what the text is mostly saying (complete sentence).\n- **Evidence** = quotation or paraphrase you can point to.\n- **Inference** = a conclusion backed by text + reasoning (not a random guess).\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a family dinner conversation about school. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 4, set 11.\n\n**Mentor passage:**\n\n> Trail crews near Tyler mark muddy sections with simple wooden signs. Hikers who slow down at those markers protect both the path and the plants beside it. A short pause also helps younger walkers notice roots and loose rocks. Rangers say most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile back to the parking lot.\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the task** in your own words (main idea? craft? integration?).\n**Step 2 — Annotate:** underline claims, circle key words, star evidence.\n**Step 3 — Answer with a complete sentence** that includes a pointer to the text.\n**Step 4 — Check:** Could a partner find your evidence in under 10 seconds?\n\n**Sample response quality:** “The central idea is that careful hikers protect trails by slowing down at muddy markers, which is supported by the detail that most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Finding Explicit Evidence,” but switch the context to a Tyler-area nature walk journaling stop. Use a fresh short example (numbers 5 and 24 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a topic (friendship) a theme or main idea.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nVocabulary and key-idea reading feed each other: you cannot summarize well if key words stay foggy.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Using the mentor passage above, apply “Finding Explicit Evidence.” Underline evidence.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Answer must point to a specific line or phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Partner challenge: write one wrong answer that looks tempting, then explain the trap.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Naming traps builds metacognition.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n2. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n3. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n4. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n5. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n6. Question: What do rangers say about injuries? Answer with a paraphrase AND a short quotation from the informational trail text ideas.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n2. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n3. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n4. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n5. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n6. Paraphrase + quote idea: most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Finding Explicit Evidence” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a topic (friendship) a theme…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about the mentor passage that depends on “Finding Explicit Evidence.” Label C / E / R in the margin. Then invent a harder follow-up question for a classmate.\n",
