    content: "# Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 4 of 16 · Grammar: Nouns · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** form regular and irregular plurals accurately.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nIn one minute, write two sentences about a Grade 6 book-club meeting. Underline every word that might be doing the job taught in “Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals.”\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 3 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nGrammar is a clarity toolkit. Today’s focus — **Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals** — helps readers trust your meaning. In Grade 6 we practice noticing patterns, naming them precisely, and revising for a real audience (a teacher, a scholarship reader, a teammate).\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Name the job** of a word (noun, pronoun, verb, modifier) before you argue about taste.\n- **Agreement** means forms match in number and person.\n- **Revision** beats guessing: change one broken piece, then re-read aloud.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a Grade 6 book-club meeting. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 10, set 2.\n\n**Mentor sentence:** “Because the evidence was incomplete, the jury asked for more time.”\n\n**Step 1 — Find the structure.** Dependent clause + comma + independent clause.\n**Step 2 — Apply today’s rule for “Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals.”** Mark the words doing that job.\n**Step 3 — Test a broken version.** What happens if we delete a key word or change agreement?\n**Step 4 — Revise a weak student sentence** so it follows the rule and still sounds natural.\n\n**Model talk:** “I checked the subject and the verb. They match. Then I read the sentence aloud to catch any leftover awkwardness.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals,” but switch the context to a Tyler-area nature walk journaling stop. Use a fresh short example (numbers 2 and 20 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a topic (friendship) a theme or main idea.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGrammar clarity supports reading claims — pronouns and verbs often carry who did what.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Label or revise for “Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals” in: “The team of scholars present their CER paragraphs after advisory.” Show each step.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Focus on the exact grammar job; explain the choice in a phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same skill, new sentence about a Grade 6 book-club meeting. Explain why your revision is clearer.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Clarity + correctness beat ornamental wording.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n2. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n3. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n4. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n5. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n6. Form plurals: box, child, tomato, deer, thesis (Grade-friendly: use *theses* or rewrite). Then use two in a sentence about school.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n2. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n3. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n4. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n5. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n6. boxes, children, tomatoes, deer, theses. Sentences will vary.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Singular, Plural, and Irregular Plurals” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a topic (friendship) a theme…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nFind a paragraph in your own writing (or invent 6–8 sentences about a Grade 6 book-club meeting). Annotate today’s grammar target, revise two places, and write a 3-sentence reflection on how clarity improved.\n",
