    title: "Subject-Verb Agreement",
    description: "Make verbs agree with subjects, including tricky cases.",
    objectives: "• Make verbs agree with subjects, including tricky cases.\n• Explain the idea with a clear example, annotation, or revision.\n• Check work: evidence pointer, agreement/clarity, or context-clue verification.",
    content: "# Subject-Verb Agreement\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 6 of 16 · Grammar: Verbs · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** make verbs agree with subjects, including tricky cases.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nIn one minute, write two sentences about a church youth reading circle. Underline every word that might be doing the job taught in “Subject-Verb Agreement.”\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 5 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nGrammar is a clarity toolkit. Today’s focus — **Subject-Verb Agreement** — helps readers trust your meaning. In Grade 6 we practice noticing patterns, naming them precisely, and revising for a real audience (a teacher, a scholarship reader, a teammate).\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Name the job** of a word (noun, pronoun, verb, modifier) before you argue about taste.\n- **Agreement** means forms match in number and person.\n- **Revision** beats guessing: change one broken piece, then re-read aloud.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a church youth reading circle. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 6, set 1.\n\n**Mentor sentence:** “Because the evidence was incomplete, the jury asked for more time.”\n\n**Step 1 — Find the structure.** Dependent clause + comma + independent clause.\n**Step 2 — Apply today’s rule for “Subject-Verb Agreement.”** Mark the words doing that job.\n**Step 3 — Test a broken version.** What happens if we delete a key word or change agreement?\n**Step 4 — Revise a weak student sentence** so it follows the rule and still sounds natural.\n\n**Model talk:** “I checked the subject and the verb. They match. Then I read the sentence aloud to catch any leftover awkwardness.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Subject-Verb Agreement,” but switch the context to an East Texas library quiet hour. Use a fresh short example (numbers 1 and 16 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a topic (friendship) a theme or main idea.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGrammar clarity supports reading claims — pronouns and verbs often carry who did what.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Label or revise for “Subject-Verb Agreement” in: “The team of scholars present their CER paragraphs after advisory.” Show each step.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Focus on the exact grammar job; explain the choice in a phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Same skill, new sentence about a church youth reading circle. Explain why your revision is clearer.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Clarity + correctness beat ornamental wording.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n2. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n3. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n4. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n5. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n6. Fix agreement: “Each scholar submitted their outline late.” Offer two acceptable Grade 6 revisions (singular generic vs rephrase plural).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n2. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n3. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n4. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n5. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n6. Each scholar submitted his or her outline / All scholars submitted their outlines / use the student’s name.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Subject-Verb Agreement” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a topic (friendship) a theme…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nFind a paragraph in your own writing (or invent 6–8 sentences about a church youth reading circle). Annotate today’s grammar target, revise two places, and write a 3-sentence reflection on how clarity improved.\n",
