    content: "# Evaluating an Argument\n\n*Grade 6 English Language Arts · Unit 12 of 16 · Reading: Integration of Knowledge and Ideas · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** judge whether reasons and evidence are sufficient and fair.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a choice works (a word, a sentence, a piece of evidence), not only label it. Prefer complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSkim this mini-passage once, then write one sentence naming what it seems to be mostly about:\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.…\n\nTurn and talk: Which earlier habit from Unit 11 might help today?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nReading is thinking with a text. Today’s skill — **Evaluating an Argument** — sits inside Reading: Integration of Knowledge and Ideas. We will slow down, annotate, and back every claim with evidence a classmate could find.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nAt Prosper Prep, clear reading and writing support scholarship habits: follow directions, cite evidence, and revise until a stranger can follow your thinking. Families use these skills when reading news, contracts, team emails, and faith or community texts. East Texas students deserve elite literacy tools — not tricks.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Main idea / central idea** = what the text is mostly saying (complete sentence).\n- **Evidence** = quotation or paraphrase you can point to.\n- **Inference** = a conclusion backed by text + reasoning (not a random guess).\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a Friday athletic study hall. Related practice numbers/labels you may see in items: set 4, set 4.\n\n**Mentor passage:**\n\n> Maya paused at the mailbox. The envelope was thin, but her name looked official. She did not open it on the porch. Inside, she set it on the kitchen table and washed her hands first, as if cleanliness could calm the shaking in her fingers. When she finally slid a thumb under the flap, she found three sentences and a deadline. Hope, she realized, could be heavy.\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the task** in your own words (main idea? craft? integration?).\n**Step 2 — Annotate:** underline claims, circle key words, star evidence.\n**Step 3 — Answer with a complete sentence** that includes a pointer to the text.\n**Step 4 — Check:** Could a partner find your evidence in under 10 seconds?\n\n**Sample response quality:** “The central idea is that careful hikers protect trails by slowing down at muddy markers, which is supported by the detail that most injuries happen when people rush the last half mile.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the skill of “Evaluating an Argument,” but switch the context to a church youth reading circle. Use a fresh short example (numbers 5 and 15 if you invent a list or ranking).\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Show the markings (parts of speech, clues, or annotations).\n3. Produce the answer or revision.\n4. Add a concluding sentence that names the skill.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature answers.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** capitalizing school subjects that are not proper nouns (except languages).\n- **Also watch for:** copying a whole paragraph as “evidence” instead of selecting the precise line, or fixing grammar by making sentences longer and muddier.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then revise only from that step.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nIntegration asks you to use key ideas + craft across more than one source — CER is the glue.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 —** Using the mentor passage above, apply “Evaluating an Argument.” Underline evidence.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Answer must point to a specific line or phrase.\n\n2. **Guided 2 —** Partner challenge: write one wrong answer that looks tempting, then explain the trap.\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Naming traps builds metacognition.\n\n3. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “Because it says so.” What follow-up question should you ask before accepting that response?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible thinking (annotations, labels, or revisions).\n\n1. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n2. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n3. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n4. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n5. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n6. Label fact vs opinion: (1) “Many middle-school students naturally fall asleep later.” (2) “Early bells are unfair.” Then rewrite (2) as a checkable claim.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Prefer complete sentences and marked evidence or revisions.*\n\n1. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n2. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n3. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n4. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n5. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n6. (1) factual claim needing evidence; (2) opinion/loaded. Rewrite: measurable effects on sleep/attendance.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Evaluating an Argument” help a reader or writer do?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to capitalizing school subjects that are not…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about the mentor passage that depends on “Evaluating an Argument.” Label C / E / R in the margin. Then invent a harder follow-up question for a classmate.\n",
